    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=DEA00000510": {
      "title": "Chief Development Officer",
      "description": "Job Description - Chief Development Officer (DEA00000510) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Chief Development Officer (DEA00000510) Job Description Chief Development Officer - ( DEA00000510 ) Description Chief Development Officer Searches Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, Cox School of Business, and Dedman School of Law Southern Methodist University Dallas, Texas THE SEARCHES Southern Methodist University (SMU) seeks three accomplished, strategic, and collaborative advancement leaders to serve as the inaugural Chief Development Officers (CDOs) for the Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , and the Dedman School of Law . These newly created positions represent a significant investment in the future of philanthropy at SMU and underscore the university’s commitment to building a best-in-class advancement enterprise capable of supporting its ambitious academic and institutional aspirations. Southern Methodist University is experiencing a period of exceptional momentum. Under the leadership of President Jay Hartzell and Senior Vice President for Development and External Affairs Shontel Grumhaus, the university is reimagining its advancement model and investing in the people, processes, and infrastructure needed to elevate fundraising performance across the institution. The creation of these three executive-level positions marks the first phase of that transformation and reflects a commitment to building a more sophisticated, collaborative, and donor-centered culture of philanthropy. Reporting to the Vice President for Schools and Unit Development, Paula Voyles Jewell, and serving as a senior strategic partner to their respective dean, each CDO will lead the development strategy for a major academic unit while helping shape the future of advancement at SMU. These leaders will have the opportunity to establish new standards for donor engagement, stewardship, prospect strategy, and team development while working alongside deans who are deeply committed to philanthropy and eager to expand their external engagement efforts. The opportunities across these three academic units are substantial. The Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, the university’s largest and most academically diverse academic unit, presents extensive opportunities to deepen donor engagement across its 16 departments and nine interdisciplinary institutes and centers. Following an extensive renovation and expansion project, the Cox School of Business is building on strong donor engagement and recent fundraising success to support ambitious priorities in faculty excellence, entrepreneurship, technology, experiential learning, and business innovation. Similarly, the Dedman School of Law is advancing a transformative capital initiative for a new law school facility on the university’s growing East Campus while cultivating a highly accomplished alumni base with significant philanthropic capacity. As inaugural leaders in these roles, the CDOs will inherit exceptional philanthropic potential. SMU boasts visionary institutional and advancement leadership, a deeply loyal alumni community, influential volunteer leaders, and access to one of the country’s most dynamic metropolitan regions. University leadership believes the institution possesses extraordinary untapped fundraising opportunity and is thereby making a deliberate investment to realize that potential. The successful candidates will be accomplished fundraisers and collaborative leaders who bring strategic vision, intellectual curiosity, and a record of building strong partnerships across complex organizations. They will be energized by the opportunity to help shape a growing advancement enterprise, serve as trusted advisors to academic leadership, and play defining roles in the next chapter of philanthropic success at Southern Methodist University. Southern Methodist University has retained Jack Gorman of the national executive search firm Isaacson, Miller to conduct these important recruitments. Confidential inquiries, nominations, and applications may be directed to the search firm as indicated at the end of this posting. ABOUT Southern Methodist University A nationally ranked private university located near the heart of Dallas, Southern Methodist University is a distinguished center for global research and teaching. SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the university’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study, and innovative programs. The university serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, Cox School of Business, Lyle School of Engineering, Meadows School of the Arts, Simmons School of Education and Human Development, Dedman School of Law, Perkins School of Theology, and Mood School of Graduate and Advanced Studies. SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the university to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty, and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers learning, research, social, and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. Fundraising and Financial Overview The Office of Development and External Affairs (DEA) comprises 147 professionals and supports Southern Methodist University by securing funding for university priorities; promoting meaningful involvement in the life and work of the university; heightening international, national, state, and local awareness of SMU; and supporting the enrollment of a diverse and able student body. In Fiscal Year 2026, SMU raised over $300 million in philanthropic support. From 2017 to 2025, SMU Ignited: Boldly Shaping Tomorrow – the university’s campaign for impact – inspired an era of unprecedented advancement in academics, athletics, and national prominence. Thanks to more than 67,500 donors, volunteers, and supporters, the campaign exceeded its goal three years ahead of schedule, raising over $1.656 billion and becoming one of the largest campaigns in the history of private Texas universities. KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES The Chief Development Officers serve as the senior advancement leaders across three respective academic units: the Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, the Cox School of Business, and the Dedman School of Law. The CDOs report directly to Paula Voyles Jewell, Vice President for Schools and Unit Development, and serve as trusted advisors to their respective deans. In these newly created positions, the CDOs will set the strategic direction for development efforts for these three prominent academic units; manage and grow major gifts portfolios; and engage key constituents in fundraising efforts. As SMU invests in its advancement operation in preparation for its next comprehensive campaign, the CDOs will also strategically grow the fundraising teams for these academic units over time. · Strategic Fundraising Leadership: Develop and implement a comprehensive fundraising strategy aligned with the academic unit’s strategic plan and university priorities. Establish annual fundraising goals, campaign objectives, and performance metrics while identifying opportunities to increase philanthropic support. · Major and Principal Gift Fundraising: Personally manage a portfolio of high-capacity prospects and donors. Cultivate, solicit, and steward major and principal gifts through strategic engagement, proposal development, and collaborative donor strategies with university leadership and the dean. · Partnership with the Dean: Serve as the dean’s primary strategic advisor on philanthropy. Partner with the dean to identify fundraising priorities, prepare donor strategies, coordinate cultivation activities, and strengthen relationships with alumni, friends, corporations, and foundations. · Leadership and Staff Management: Provide leadership, coaching, supervision, and performance management for development professionals supporting the school or college. Establish performance expectations, develop staff, promote collaboration, and foster a culture of accountability and excellence. · Volunteer and Advisory Board Engagement: Partner with advisory boards, campaign committees, volunteers, and key constituents to strengthen philanthropic engagement and expand fundraising opportunities. · Campaign and Strategic Initiatives: Lead the academic unit’s participation in comprehensive campaigns and strategic fundraising initiatives. Coordinate campaign planning, volunteer engagement, and fundraising strategies to achieve campaign goals. · Collaboration Across Development and External Affairs: Work collaboratively with principal gifts, corporate and foundation relations, gift planning, prospect development, alumni relations, donor relations, annual giving, and other university partners to maximize donor engagement and philanthropic outcomes. · Financial Management and Reporting: Monitor fundraising performance, analyze philanthropic trends, prepare reports and forecasts, and manage departmental budgets and fundraising resources. · University Leadership and Committee Participation: Represent the school or college and Development and External Affairs (DEA) on university committees and leadership initiatives while promoting collaboration across academic and administrative units. APPLICATIONS, INQUIRIES, AND NOMINATIONS Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search processes. Inquiries, nominations, referrals, and CVs with cover letters should be sent via the Isaacson, Miller website: Chief Development Officers, Southern Methodist University Jack Gorman, Partner Nicole Poe, Managing Associate Grace Zakim, Managing Associate Max Bartley, Associate Hodge Stillwell, Associate Ryan Cheung, Senior Search Coordinator Isaacson, Miller Southern Methodist University will not discriminate in any employment practice, education program, education activity, or admissions on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. SMU’s commitment to equal opportunity includes nondiscrimination on the basis of sexual orientation and gender identity and expression. This document has been prepared based on the information provided by Southern Methodist University. The material presented in this leadership profile should be relied on for informational purposes only. While every effort has been made to ensure the accuracy of this information, the original source documents and information provided by SMU would supersede any conflicting information in this document. Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS AND CHARACTERISTICS · A minimum of ten years of professional fundraising experience and at least five years of management experience, preferably within a private research university setting. · Depth of knowledge of best practices in all key areas of development and donor engagement strategies; demonstrated campaign experience. · Demonstrated ability and experience building upon and/or transforming an existing development program. · A proven track record of success in personally cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding gifts at the six- and seven-figure levels and above. A high level of comfort and effectiveness in working with high-net-worth individuals. · Proven leadership and solid volunteer and staff management skills; desire to empower and motivate staff while providing the support and advocacy they need, as well as the ability to influence others beyond formal authority. · Exemplary interpersonal communication skills; active, engaged listener; superior written and oral communication skills; clarity, crispness, and elegance in writing and speaking. The ability to succinctly articulate a vision and processes to key constituents, utilizing data and metrics as appropriate. · The credibility, emotional maturity, authenticity, and sound judgment required to effectively engage and leverage university and academic leadership and volunteers in the cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of key prospects and donors. · The desire and ability to build bridges and strong collaborative relationships with all members of an operation. · A sharp eye for operational efficiency and the best use of resources. · The ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and with confidence and discretion. · Impeccable attention to detail; superb follow-through; strong personal integrity, charisma, and work ethic coupled with a sense of humor and perspective. · The personal flexibility to travel and work evenings or weekends as required. · Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree preferred. Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Development Organization : DEA - Development/Alumni Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Director Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 11, 2026, 10:25:45 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=DIV00000240": {
      "title": "University Catalog Editor (HR Title: Content Editor I)",
      "description": "Job Description - University Catalog Editor (HR Title: Content Editor I) (DIV00000240) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - University Catalog Editor (HR Title: Content Editor I) (DIV00000240) Job Description University Catalog Editor (HR Title: Content Editor I) - ( DIV00000240 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The University Catalog Editor leads the development and publication of the University's annual academic catalog, serving as the primary resource for catalog policies, processes, and content standards. Working collaboratively with academic and administrative stakeholders, this role ensures that programs, courses, policies, and institutional information are accurate, current, and aligned with approved curricular changes. The Catalog Editor manages the catalog production cycle, oversees content within the catalog management system, and delivers a high-quality, accessible publication that supports student success and institutional excellence. Essential Functions: Coordinate and manage the annual academic catalog production cycle, including developing and maintaining the catalog production calendar, establishing deadlines for content submission and review, coordinating editing and approval activities, and ensuring the catalog is completed and published according to established timelines. Oversee the development, editing, and production of the University's annual academic catalog, coordinating with schools, academic departments, and administrative offices to initiate, review, edit, and finalize catalog content. Ensure published on University website and content is accurate, consistent, complete, and compliant with established University editorial and publication standards. Coordinate the Catalog Advisory Committee, including establishing meeting schedules, developing agendas, coordinating the review of catalog content and proposed changes, documenting recommendations, and facilitating communication and follow-up with participating academic and administrative units. Utilize and administer the Coursedog Curriculum Management and Catalog modules to manage, review, edit, & publish curriculum and catalog content. Monitor the integration of approved curriculum information into the catalog and identify & resolve discrepancies between curriculum records, catalog content, & related University systems. Work with software vendor as needed. Train users and test functionality. Collaborate with the Manager of Academic Programs to ensure curriculum changes reflected in the academic catalog have completed the appropriate University academic and governing approval processes prior to publication. Review proposed curriculum changes for appropriate approvals and coordinate with academic units to resolve questions or discrepancies. Coordinate the review and maintenance of course descriptions and other academic content appearing in the academic catalog and my.SMU, working with school and departmental representatives to ensure information is accurate, current, and consistent across systems. Maintain and coordinate updates to catalog sections managed by offices and groups outside the Registrar's Office, including institutional policies, financial information, transfer information, and other University-wide content. Conduct quality assurance and final reviews to ensure the published catalog accurately reflects approved University information. Evening and weekend work will be required of all Registrar's Office employees to work at academic ceremonies and may be required to meet deadlines. Qualifications Education and Experience: Bachelor’s is required. A minimum of two (2) years of experience in writing and editing is required. Administrative experience in a college or university is also required. Experience editing academic publications is preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong writing skills, grammar and usage, as well as editing and oral communication skills. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and understand inconsistencies, issues, and potential problems in policies, text, and presentation. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time management skills.  Experience managing multiple projects in a fast-paced team environment with multiple deadlines is required. Candidate must be familiar with university policies, procedures and academic programs and have experience working in an active, customer service-oriented environment. Must possess the ability to work well as part of a team and in an independent role. Demonstrated knowledge of and experience with desktop publishing platforms and sufficient computer experience to demonstrate ability to learn new software is preferred. Experience using course scheduling, curriculum changes, and online catalog systems (Coursedog) will be helpful for success in this role. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Stand Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be granted to applications received by October 2, 2026. Position will remain open until filled. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Other Organization : Division of Enrollment Services Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 11, 2026, 7:55:26 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=DED00000495": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant to Registrar & Faculty (HR title: Administrative Assistant 2)",
      "description": "Job Description - Administrative Assistant to Registrar & Faculty (HR title: Administrative Assistant 2) (DED00000495) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Administrative Assistant to Registrar & Faculty (HR title: Administrative Assistant 2) (DED00000495) Job Description Administrative Assistant to Registrar & Faculty (HR title: Administrative Assistant 2) - ( DED00000495 ) Description Hourly Range: $24.70 per hour About SMU: SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: Perform a wide variety of administrative tasks for the Registrar's Office and several faculty members at the law school, including clerical support, processing forms, publication support and editing work, travel planning, meeting set up logistics, scheduling, processing reimbursements, exam support, coordinating makeup exams, tracking of final exams, and other administrative duties. Essential Functions: (Faculty) Provide admin support to faculty and adjuncts, inc. assistance w/ academic and course-related needs, word processing, calendaring, databases, and research; provide clerical support inc. keyboarding, composing and editing letters, copying, scanning, maintaining filing, responding to emails, ordering textbooks, and disseminating info; edit faculty articles for publications; mail reprints. (Faculty) Arranges meetings and travel arrangements for conferences, seminars, symposiums, and other events; coordinates travel reimbursement and other expense reimbursements. (Faculty) Working with faculty and other staff, coordinates dissemination of and tracking of final exams, and provides information to students and faculty regarding final exams as directed. (Registrar) Prepare and process a variety of academic and administrative forms, letters, and certifications, including Character and Fitness documentation for bar examination applicants, letters of good standing, class rank certifications, and documentation for external organizations and agencies; coordinate and maintain student records through document management activities, including scanning. (Registrar) Create, distribute, track, and process electronic forms and approvals through DocuSign for a variety of academic programs and student requests, including clinic enrollment, directed research, externships, law review participation, advocacy program enrollment, study-away petitions, application amendments, graduate-level course requests, and withdrawal requests. (Registrar) Coordinate scheduling and administration of make-up examinations. (Registrar) Prepare and process transfer documentation and related materials for students seeking to transfer to other institutions. (Registrar) Assist with registration-related processes and maintain accurate records to ensure compliance with institutional policies, procedures, and deadlines, including comparing information in my.SMU to website and materials distributed to students. (Registrar) Respond to inquiries from students, faculty, staff, alumni, and external organizations regarding academic records and administrative processes. (Miscellaneous) Covers time out of the office for other administrative assistants; provides overflow support to other administrative assistants as needed; perform other duties and special projects as assigned. Qualifications Education and Experience: A high school diploma or equivalent is required. Bachelor's degree is preferred. A minimum of two (2) years of work experience in an administrative assistant or similar support role in a high-volume setting is required. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidates must demonstrate strong interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively and broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies, including students, faculty, and staff. Candidates must be able to maintain paper and electronic files, work independently, anticipate issues, and work effectively under the supervision of several faculty members, as well as the Assistant Dean for Administration. Candidates must possess strong problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify and analyze problems and devise solutions. Candidates must also have strong organizational, planning, and time-management skills. Candidates must demonstrate high-level competency with the Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, and PowerPoint). Candidates must also have the ability and willingness to learn new systems as they are implemented. Knowledge of Publisher and Adobe Acrobat is preferred. Physical and Environmental Demands: Bend Carry/lift 25 lbs. Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: September 24, 2026 For full consideration candidate must submit a resume or CV; and a cover letter expressing why you are interested in the position and your relevant experience. EEO Statement: SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Office and Administrative Support Organization : Dedman School of Law Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 10, 2026, 11:50:04 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=DIV00000242": {
      "title": "Business System Specialist",
      "description": "Job Description - Business System Specialist (DIV00000242) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Business System Specialist (DIV00000242) Job Description Business System Specialist - ( DIV00000242 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. This position is part of the management team for the Registrar’s office and provides support for the student athletes’ academic certification, Degree Program Module, ad hoc queries and Release of Education Records in Student Administrative System (my.SMU) for the Division of Enrollment Services and other university constituents. In the respective capacity of this position, contribute to the Registrar’s Office efforts to support the SMU Quality Enhancement Plan “SMU-in-FOUR”. Essential Functions: Student Athletes’ Academic Certification: Interpret NCAA and Conference ACC (Atlantic Coast Conference) academic eligibility regulations for student athletes. Interpret SMU academic progress policies and implement appropriate procedures with regard to student athletes. Serve as the Registrar’s liaison with schools to track academic progress toward degree. Monitor academic eligibility for first year, continuing, and transfer student athletes and verify satisfactory academic progress. Manage documentation for Athletic Certification processes which includes writing new and modifying existing policies and procedures. Identify and create data queries to support and enhance Athletic Certification processes. Responsible for collecting, reporting and maintaining Academic Program Progress (APP) data in NCAA system. Manage access to NCAA single sign-on for SMU. Troubleshoot issues and problems with Student Athletes processes in my.SMU and NCAA system. Provide training to fellow RO team members. Degree Program Module: Compare approved new/changes to requirements to the catalog for accuracy. Setup for Academic Advisement Module requirements, requirement groups, course list, and other required entities to run Academic Advisement accurately and efficiently using SMU’s bulletin. Maintain requirements for Academic Advisement and Degree Planner. Set up and maintain Highpoint degree paths for academic plans. Maintain documentation for Academic Advisement and Degree Planner. Respond to inquiries and helpdesk tickets for Academic Advisement and Degree Planner. Image supporting documentation for changes made to the Academic Advisement setup. Provides system support to all academic areas. Troubleshoots issues and problems in the Academic Advisement module and the Degree Planner module. Write Query for AA-related requests. Ad hoc Queries: Write/build queries as requested by users. Run preformatted queries as requested by users. Research request and coordinate with technology staff to identify source of data and process needed to fill request. Maintain queries created and make modifications as needed. Document purpose of each query, explain how statistics are obtained, and how to run the process. Support for Release of Education Records (RER/FERPA): Release of Education Records & FERPA. Setup for Release of Education Records. Provide system support for the Release of Education Records. Troubleshoots issues and problems with Release of Education Records. Participate in and ensures quality testing for all updates, fixes, and enhancements installation. Accept special project assignments. General Responsibilities: Attend meetings/conferences to stay abreast of technology issues. Attend all Division and Team meetings. Attend staff development and training activities. Assist with Academic Ceremonies. Assist and support other Registrar activities and projects as well as Division-wide activities as requested. Responsible for knowing, understanding and following about the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act of 1974 (FERPA. Responsible for knowing and adhering to the Gramm, Leach, Bliley Act was passed in the United States Congress in 1999 • Responsible for abiding by the rules and regulations of the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA). Occasional evening/weekend hours may be required for projects and ceremonies. Qualifications Education and Experience: A Bachelor’s degree is required. A minimum of three (3) years of computer related work experience is required. Experience with an institution of Higher Education, PeopleSoft Student Administrative System and student athlete certification is preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time management skills. Candidate must be able to demonstrate knowledge of MS Word, Excel, Access, and Outlook. Ability to learn new software including Query Tools is required. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by September 24, 2026. Application deadline is October 8, 2026. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Other Organization : Division of Enrollment Services Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 10, 2026, 9:58:02 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=MEA00000166": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean for Recruiting & Admissions",
      "description": "Job Description - Assistant Dean for Recruiting & Admissions (MEA00000166) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Assistant Dean for Recruiting & Admissions (MEA00000166) Job Description Assistant Dean for Recruiting & Admissions - ( MEA00000166 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Department: SMU Meadows School of the Arts educates visionary artists, scholars, and arts and communications professionals to create sustainable and transformative impact on both local and global society. Today, the Meadows School has achieved prominence as one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States and offers training in an unique mix of the arts – visual (art and art history), performing (dance, music, and theatre) and communications (advertising, film and media arts, corporate communication and public affairs, and journalism) – as well as a preeminent programs in arts management and arts entrepreneurship and creative computation. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The position serves as the senior leader responsible for the vision, strategy, and execution of all undergraduate and graduate recruitment and admissions for the Meadows School of the Arts. With full accountability for enrollment outcomes across diverse programs, including dual-admission disciplines, the position exercises significant autonomy in shaping recruitment strategy, admissions processes, and financial aid leveraging. Working closely with the Dean, faculty leadership, and university partners, the position aligns academic priorities with enrollment goals. This role leads a data-informed approach to recruitment, using analytics and market intelligence to guide decision-making, strengthen Meadows’ national position, and ensure the successful recruitment of top-tier students. Essential Functions: Strategic Leadership & Accountability - Provide vision, leadership, and full accountability for recruitment and admissions outcomes across all undergraduate and graduate Meadows programs, including dual-admission disciplines and direct-admit majors, aligning enrollment strategy with school and university priorities. Enrollment Strategy & Execution - Design and implement a comprehensive, data-driven recruitment and admissions strategy, including application generation, yield optimization, and financial aid leveraging, with authority to adjust tactics in response to market conditions and enrollment performance. Admissions Process Oversight - Direct and oversee all Meadows admissions processes, ensuring consistency, efficiency, and alignment with institutional policies, while supporting discipline-specific requirements such as auditions, portfolios, and faculty review. Faculty & Academic Partnership - Lead and influence division chairs, program directors, and faculty to develop and execute discipline-specific recruitment strategies that align with school-wide enrollment goals and support the shaping of incoming classes. Data, Analytics & Market Intelligence - Leverage data, analytics, and market research to inform strategy, monitor performance, and provide actionable insights to the Dean and academic leadership, ensuring continuous improvement and competitive positioning. Team Leadership & Operations - Lead, manage, and develop a high-performing recruitment and admissions team, establishing clear goals, accountability measures, and professional development opportunities to support operational excellence. Marketing, Outreach & Representation - Oversee recruitment communications, events, and outreach in partnership with Marketing, and represent Meadows at institutional and national levels to enhance visibility, build relationships, and expand the prospective student pipeline. Cross-Functional Collaboration & Resource Management - Collaborate with university partners (Admissions, Financial Aid, Advising, Marketing) to ensure seamless processes and student experience, and oversee recruitment-related resources and budgets to maximize impact and return on investment. Occasional evening/weekend hours will be required. Frequent travel will be required for student recruitment and conferences. Qualifications Education and Experience: A Master's degree required. A degree in Arts (performing or visual), Communications or Education or is preferred. A minimum six (6) years of progressively responsible experience in higher education admissions, recruitment, or enrollment management, including experience in arts or specialized admissions environments is required. Experience must include direct work in specialized admissions environments (arts preferred), not solely general admissions. Experience managing budgets, financial aid leveraging strategies, or resource allocation in support of enrollment goal is preferred. A minimum four (4) years of leadership experience, including supervision of professional staff and management of complex teams or functions is required. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time management skills. Candidate must be able to demonstrate strategic leadership and decision-making authority in complex academic environments. Candidate must have advanced analytical skills with ability to translate data into actionable enrollment strategies. Candidate must possess a deep understanding of enrollment trends and competitive landscape. Candidate needs a strong understanding of dual-admission or portfolio/audition-based admissions processes. Candidate must be able to collaborate with and influence faculty and senior academic leadership. Candidate must have proficiency with admissions CRM systems (e.g., Slate) and data analysis tools. Advanced skills in Microsoft Excel, Word, and Outlook are necessary. Candidate must be familiar with data visualization and reporting tools to support decision-making. Ability to learn and leverage new technologies and systems that enhance recruitment, admissions operations, and enrollment strategy is a plus. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Bend, climb, stand, kneel Carry/lift 25 lbs. Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by September 30, 2026. This position is open until filled. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Administrative Managers & Professionals Organization : Meadows School of the Arts Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Manager with Direct Reports Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 9, 2026, 1:32:11 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=FAC00000237": {
      "title": "Office Coordinator (HR Title: Coordinator1)",
      "description": "Job Description - Office Coordinator (HR Title: Coordinator1) (FAC00000237) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Office Coordinator (HR Title: Coordinator1) (FAC00000237) Job Description Office Coordinator (HR Title: Coordinator1) - ( FAC00000237 ) Description Hourly Range: $23.46 per hour About SMU: SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: This role drives the daily administrative and operational efficiency of the Facilities Planning and Management department. Serving as the primary point of contact, you will manage front desk operations, greet internal and external guests, direct inquiries, perform financial reconciliations, and coordinate administrative workflows. The ideal candidate thrives in a fast-paced, detail-oriented environment. This position balances exceptional customer service with strong technical and organizational skills to ensure the long-term success of our Maintenance and Grounds operations. Ultimately, you will uphold high service standards to help fulfill the broader mission of Facilities Planning and Management and the University. Essential Functions: Provide comprehensive administrative assistance to the Support AVP and their direct reports. Manage calendars, coordinate schedules, and proactively resolve scheduling conflicts. Organize and set up department-wide meetings, presentations, and vendor meetings. Process daily key returns and key issuance to requesters, internal staff, and external vendors. Sort and deliver incoming mail and packages. Order copy paper, coffee, and utensils on a monthly basis. Work with vendors regarding vending machine updates, issues, and storage. Format, update, and distribute departmental policies, standard operating procedures, and other documentation. Generate and manage monthly on-call schedules for both technicians and managers. Maintain an updated master database of all personnel records. Track employee details, including Employee IDs, start dates, and birthdays. Log and audit company assets issued to staff, including lockers, iPads, and radios. Support Business Operations Coordinators with information gathering, tracking, reporting, and transaction-related data to support analysis of computer database submissions and general facilities or construction projects, as needed. Support the Call Center, as needed, with escalations regarding calls or work orders. Assist with troubleshooting and triaging facilities-related calls and work requests, as needed. Ability to work additional hours as needed during high-demand and seasonal peak periods, including games, graduations, move-in and move-out days, and inclement weather events. Perform other duties as assigned by Leadership. Qualifications Education and Experience: A high school diploma or equivalent is required. Bachelor's degree is preferred. A minimum of two (2) years of work experience is required. Experience with work order management, vendor and contractor coordination, general administrative support, scheduling, record maintenance, and/or building access management is preferred. Experience in a customer service call center desired. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. A strong customer service orientation is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time-management skills with the ability to multitask. A strong focus on attention to detail is essential. Candidate must possess a strong working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite (e.g. Outlook, Word). Familiarity with and/or experience using MS Project, Excel, and PowerPoint preferred. Knowledge of Central Control and Monitoring Systems (CCMS), Integrated Workplace Management Systems (IWMS), iPad software, computer updates and migrations, and/or Digital App Space is preferred. Physical and Environmental Demands: Physical: Bend Sit for long periods of time Squat Handle objects (dexterity) Carry/lift over 50 lbs. Kneel Stand Walk for long distances Environmental: Exposure to excessive noise Around moving machinery Drives motorized equipment Work in confined quarters Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by September 28 , 2026. The position is open until filled. EEO Statement: SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Facilities Organization : Facilities Planning Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 14, 2026, 1:23:36 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=ORG00000043": {
      "title": "Campus Services Office Administrator (Administrative Specialist I)",
      "description": "Job Description - Campus Services Office Administrator (Administrative Specialist I) (ORG00000043) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Campus Services Office Administrator (Administrative Specialist I) (ORG00000043) Job Description Campus Services Office Administrator (Administrative Specialist I) - ( ORG00000043 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: The Campus Services Administrator will perform a wide range of administrative and office management activities for the Associate Vice President (AVP), who manages multiple departments. Facilitating efficient departmental operations and ensuring the effective delivery of services are key responsibilities of this role. This critical position serves as the informational hub for the AVP and the leadership teams across the multiple departments within Campus Services. Additionally, the Campus Services Administrator interacts with clients and guests, providing information and responding to inquiries, concerns, and requests regarding services offered by all departments within Campus Services. A high degree of creativity and latitude is expected, along with a strong level of initiative and sound judgment when handling a variety of critical and complex decisions. The ideal candidate will excel in a diverse and dynamic office environment. This role is deemed operationally critical and is an on-campus, in-person position. Working from home to provide continuity of service during inclement weather is required. Essential Functions: Greet and direct callers and in-person visitors to the appropriate Division Director, escalating matters to the Director of Operations or Associate Vice President when appropriate, using critical problem-solving skills and sound judgment. Manage the end-to-end process for the Vending, Conference Services, and Event Services mailboxes, including responding to inquiries, directing messages to appropriate team members, and escalating issues as necessary. Manage the Campus Services Executive Office by overseeing internal processes related to purchasing, building management, payroll, and SMU brand standards. Perform office management tasks, including accounts payable and receivable, budget reconciliation, student worker payroll documentation, and asset tracking. Provide comprehensive calendar management for the Associate Vice President and Director of Operations. Effectively coordinate a range of complex meetings, accurately record meeting minutes as required, and ensure all action items are followed through to completion. Initiate and manage special projects requiring cross-departmental coordination from inception to completion, utilizing proactive, high-touch communication and providing regular project updates. Manage Coca-Cola and food vending service-related matters campus-wide, including contract management, problem resolution, monthly vendor meetings, service requests, and machine location and relocation. Maintain the Campus Services, Conference Services, and Event Services websites, including updating relevant text and coordinating an annual refresh of images. Coordinate with all divisions and manage the completion of the annual assessment process, annual accomplishment reporting, and annual reports encompassing all divisions within Campus Services. Manage key functions within STABLE, including facilitating external group alcohol service approvals, processing internal work orders, and coordinating space reservations upon request. Assist in the development of key materials, including spreadsheets, PowerPoint presentations, and executive-level communication pieces for dissemination to a wide audience. Train all temporary, summer, and student workers on office procedures, office protocols, telephone etiquette, and administrative procedures necessary to complete their assignments. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications Education and Experience: A high school diploma or equivalent is required. A bachelor’s degree is preferred, preferably in Business or a related field. A minimum of 5 (five) years of professional-level work experience providing administrative support is required. Experience in project administration, budget and expense management, and providing administrative support for a high-functioning dynamic teams is preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with clients, guests, and leadership team. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. A strong customer service orientation is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving and creative thinking skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as provide innovative ideas and solutions. Must also have a collaborative manner, and a tactical approach to a variety of challenges and opportunities. Candidate must have strong organizational, planning and time-management skills with an ability to balance multiple projects/reports and deadlines. Demonstrated ability in coordinating work among multiple parties is essential. Candidate must possess a strong working knowledge of the Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook). Experience with or knowledge of a web content management system (e.g. Sitecore), or an equivalent platform is preferred. Physical and Environmental Demands: Bend Reach above shoulders Carry/lift 25 lbs. Sit for long periods of time Stand Walk for long distances Push/Pull Deadline to Apply: October 12, 2026 Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by October 2, 2026. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Office and Administrative Support Organization : Auxiliary Services Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 16, 2026, 3:38:43 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=OFF00000192": {
      "title": "Executive Briefer, Office of President or Provost",
      "description": "Job Description - Executive Briefer, Office of President or Provost (OFF00000192) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Executive Briefer, Office of President or Provost (OFF00000192) Job Description Executive Briefer, Office of President or Provost - ( OFF00000192 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: Southern Methodist University (SMU) seeks a highly skilled, service-oriented, and strategically minded Executive Briefer to support either the President or Provost. This pivotal role serves as a trusted partner responsible for preparing senior leadership for meetings, decisions, and engagements by synthesizing information into concise daily briefing materials. The Executive Briefer combines exceptional communication skills, sound judgment, and organizational excellence to ensure the executive is consistently prepared, informed, and positioned for success. In addition to executive briefing responsibilities, the position serves as a primary point of contact for the assigned executive office, helping manage access to executive leadership, coordinating information flow, and providing a welcoming and professional experience for internal and external stakeholders. The ideal candidate thrives in a fast-paced environment, exercises discretion with sensitive information, anticipates needs, and demonstrates a commitment to exceptional service. The position may require occasional early morning, evening, or weekend work based on the executive's schedule and University events. The position works primarily on campus and requires consistent in-person presence in the Executive Office Suite. Essential Functions: Executive Briefing & Preparation Prepare daily briefing materials aligned with the executive's schedule, upcoming decisions, and anticipated issues. Conduct research and gather relevant information to ensure the executive is fully informed and prepared for meetings, events, and engagements. Review, analyze, and synthesize correspondence, meeting materials, reports, requests into concise executive briefing documents. Identify information gaps, outstanding issues, and follow-up needs; coordinate with university leaders and stakeholders to obtain timely and accurate information. Front Office, Access & Executive Experience Serve as the first point of contact for the senior executive office Provide a welcoming, professional, and high-touch experience for visitors, callers, and stakeholders. Manage incoming calls, inquiries, and requests by appropriately triaging, resolving, routing, or escalating matters as appropriate. Exercise professionalism, diplomacy, and discretion when handling sensitive or confidential issues. Calendar Coordination & Daily Readiness Partner closely with the Executive Scheduler to ensure briefing materials and preparation needs align with the executive's calendar and priorities. Monitor upcoming engagements and proactively identify competing demands, preparation requirements, and urgent requests. Ensure all briefing materials, talking points, meeting documents, and supporting resources are provided accurately and on time. Assist in maintaining executive readiness by anticipating needs and facilitating the smooth flow of information. Judgment, Follow-Through & Team Collaboration Exercise sound judgment when managing sensitive, urgent, or rapidly changing matters. Track action items, commitments, and follow-up activities to ensure timely completion. Provide cross-coverage and operational support within the President's and Provost's executive offices as needed. Qualifications Education and Experience: High school diploma or equivalent and a minimum of six years of work experience OR Bachelor’s degree and a minimum of two years of wok experience is required. Progressively responsible experience in executive support, executive-office operations, chief-of-staff-adjacent work, communications, or another high-touch professional environment preferred. Experience supporting a senior executive or working in a high profile office desired. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal skills with executive presence and a service-oriented approach. Must also demonstrate strong verbal and written communication skills with a keen attention to detail and accuracy and the ability to quickly synthesize complex information into concise summaries. Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgement, discretion, confidentiality, and political acumen are essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning, and time-management skills. The ability to manage competing priorities, make sounds decisions with limited direction, and collaborate with senior leaders and internal and external constituents is essential. Physical and Environmental Demands: Bend, squat, stand, push/pull Handle objects (dexterity) Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: The position is open until filled. Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by October 2, 2026. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Administrative Managers & Professionals Organization : Office of the President Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 16, 2026, 2:20:57 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=HUM00000055": {
      "title": "Associate Director Total Rewards",
      "description": "Job Description - Associate Director Total Rewards (HUM00000055) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Associate Director Total Rewards (HUM00000055) Job Description Associate Director Total Rewards - ( HUM00000055 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: The Associate Director of Total Rewards is responsible for the strategic management, administration, and continuous evaluation of the University's compensation, classification, benefits, retirement, wellness, and related Total Rewards programs. The role focuses on aligning employee rewards with the University's strategic objectives to attract, retain, and engage a high-performing workforce while ensuring market competitiveness, operational effectiveness, and fiscal responsibility. The Associate Director contributes to the development and execution of the University's Total Rewards strategy by evaluating market trends, industry best practices, and budget considerations. This position leads the design and administration of competitive compensation programs and provides guidance on Total Rewards initiatives that support the University's talent and workforce objectives. . Essential Functions: Benefits: Oversee the design, administration, and ongoing management of health, dental, vision, retirement plans, and other employee benefits. Manage relationships with benefit vendors, insurance carriers, brokers, and third-party administrators. Oversee service delivery, performance standards, billing and reconciliation processes, and contract renewals or modifications. Identify opportunities for improved service, cost savings, and operational efficiencies. Oversee administration of leave programs (including FMLA and medical leave), retirement processes, retiree benefits, and benefits-related aspects of employee separations. Ensure a smooth and compliant experience for employees navigating these transitions. Compensation: Administer, manage and implement the University's compensation philosophy, salary structures, classification system, and compensation programs. Conduct job evaluations, market pricing analyses, and compensation studies to ensure internal equity and external competitiveness. Manage annual compensation programs, including merit increases, promotional increases, and other salary actions. Participate in and analyze external salary surveys and other market data sources. Provide guidance and recommendations regarding hiring salaries, promotions, reclassifications, organizational changes, and compensation practices. Communication & Employee Support/Education - Execute Total Rewards communication strategies through various channels that are designed to announce changes, educate, and enhance employee satisfaction. Oversee Annual Open Enrollment process with a focus on educating employees about their benefit options, ensuring easy-to-understand communication, and providing resources to help them make informed decisions. Lead the planning and execution of the University’s annual Benefits Fair, coordinating with internal partners and external vendors to deliver a high-impact, campus-wide event that promotes employee engagement and education. Collaborate with benefits administration staff to evaluate and enhance health, welfare, retirement, and wellbeing programs. Compliance & Regulatory Oversight - Ensure compliance with all relevant labor laws and regulations related to compensation and benefits (e.g., ACA, ERISA, HIPAA, FMLA. Maintain and update plan documents, summary plan descriptions, and related materials. Partner with internal and external stakeholders to monitor regulatory changes and implement required updates. Prepare, collect, and organize data for auditors assessment. Data Management, Reporting & Analytics - Analyze employee data to identify trends and make informed recommendations regarding compensation and benefits programs. Oversee data accuracy and integrity for HRIS and vendor systems. Produce regular reports on enrollment, utilization, cost trends, and leave activity. Strategic Planning & Program Design Support - Partner with CHRO and Sr. VP of Business and Finance to evaluate and enhance Total Rewards strategy. Assess market trends, industry practices, and emerging compensation and benefits strategies to identify opportunities for program enhancement, including plan design, benchmarking against peer institutions, compensation studies, and cost-benefit analysis. Lead and manage Total Rewards projects and initiatives, ensuring effective planning, implementation Management - Manage and develop benefits and compensation staff, ensuring high performance, accountability, and professional growth. Assign and prioritize work, establish quality standards, and provide training and guidance. Ensure service expectations are met, and support a collaborative and service-oriented team environment. Qualifications Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree is required. Master's preferred. A minimum of five (5) years of work experience in compensation, benefits, total rewards, or human resources is required. E xperience managing and administering comprehensive benefit and compensation programs is also required, as is experience managing/leading a professional staff. Experience conducting compensation analyses, market pricing, job evaluation, and salary administration is required. Experience working with compensation surveys and market benchmarking methodologies is also required. Experience with self-funded health plans is strongly preferred. Experience in higher education desired. Professional certification, such as Certified Employee Benefits Specialist (CEBS), Certified Compensation Professional (CCP) or SPHR is a plus. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. The ability to communicate complex information to various stakeholders is essential. Candidate must have a strong working knowledge and understanding of relevant regulations and laws applicable to benefits and compensation administration ( FMLA, HIPAA, COBRA, ADA, ACA, FLSA). Must also have knowledge of compensation principles, job evaluation methodologies, and market pricing practices. Knowledge of employee benefits and retirement plan administration is essential. Candidate must have the ability to analyze data, conduct research, and make informed and actionable recommendations. Must also have the ability to handle sensitive employee situations with empathy and a high level of integrity when handling confidential PHI (Protected Health Information) and sensitive employee data. A compassionate approach to leave administration (FMLA) and retirement transitions, making difficult processes feel manageable for our faculty and staff is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time management skills with a strong focus on attention to detail. Candidate must have the ability to negotiate with brokers and carriers to ensure SMU receives optimal pricing and service-level agreements (SLAs). Must also have strong vendor management skills and the ability to hold vendors accountable while maintaining a collaborative relationship. Knowledge of 403(b) plan administration and the ability to interface with investment committees is preferred. Candidate must have a strong working knowledge of MS Office, advanced skills in Excel is preferred. A working knowledge of an HRIS module is also required; PeopleSoft preferred. Proven ability to lead large-scale projects like open enrollment and a knack for data analytics to evaluate cost trends and program effectiveness are preferred. Candidate must be able to work evenings and/or weekends occasionally during peak seasons. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: The position is open until filled. Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by October 12, 2026 EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Administrative Managers & Professionals Organization : Human Resources Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Manager with Direct Reports Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Variable Travel : Yes, 10 % of the Time Job Posting : Sep 24, 2026, 7:13:43 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=RES00000096": {
      "title": "Residential Community Director (HR Title: Residence Hall Director)",
      "description": "Job Description - Residential Community Director (HR Title: Residence Hall Director) (RES00000096) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Residential Community Director (HR Title: Residence Hall Director) (RES00000096) Job Description Residential Community Director (HR Title: Residence Hall Director) - ( RES00000096 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The Residential Community Director (RCD) at SMU is a four year live-on position that provides leadership in developing and sustaining positive, academically-focused, residential communities. A successful RCD also accomplishes administrative functions required in managing a university residential community. The position is live-in, serves in an on-call rotation, and reports to an Associate Director for Residential Life. While our current vacancies are in Residential Commons serving mainly first-year and some second-year students, it is possible that the successful candidate is placed in one of our Upper Division communities serving sophomores, juniors, and seniors. Essential Functions: Train, supervise, and evaluate the job performance of student leaders. Conduct regularly scheduled individual and group meetings. Provide development and accountability for student leaders. Develop a personal knowledge of individual residents to better provide assistance and related support. Assist with retention efforts. Serve as a case manager through the Caring Community Connections program. Provide conflict mediation and parent interaction to resolve student issues. Assess needs of residents. Provide and facilitate programming (including at least one large-scale signature event program yearly) in support of the Residential Commons tradition to promote a positive living and learning environment that fosters a sense of belonging. Advise the programming board for your community. Lead and/or serve on one or more RLSH (and potentially Student Affairs) committees. Meet regularly with the supervisor and other RLSH staff. Collaborate with the Faculty in Residence (FIR) for your community and support their connection to the leadership team and programmatic efforts where applicable. Serve as a Conduct Officer for student conduct cases occurring in the residential areas. Coordinate building operations. Assist with occupancy management, including but not limited to managing room and hall changes, check-in and check-out activity, etc. Monitor the physical condition of assigned areas and report work requests and desired facility improvements per established procedures. Manage budget & financial paperwork, including purchasing and expense reports. Assist in major annual processes, e.g., staff selection, assessments, and serve on the on-call rotation to manage student and facilities emergencies, etc. Interview and hire Resident Assistants (RA)s. Responsible for managing their performance evaluations, assigning tasks, directing work, as well as discipline and discharge when necessary. Perform related duties as assigned or required to meet RLSH and University goals. This person will serve on an on-call rotation to support student meetings, programs, trainings and other events that may be required on during evenings and weekends. This person is expected to be a presence in the community in which they live. Qualifications Education and Experience: Bachelor’s is required. Master's is preferred. A degree field in Higher Education Administration, College Student Personnel, Counseling in Higher Education, or related field is preferred. Previous experience in Student Affairs or related areas is required. Previous experience working in Residence Life (full-time or graduate), working in a Residential College setting, working with academic and faculty partners and/or supervising student staff is strongly preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University as well as establish rapport and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication and listening skills. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise creative solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time management skills. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be granted to applications received by October 6, 2026. Position will remain open until filled. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Other Organization : Residence Life/Housing Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 22, 2026, 10:12:36 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=DEA00000509": {
      "title": "Vice President for Principal Giving",
      "description": "Job Description - Vice President for Principal Giving (DEA00000509) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Vice President for Principal Giving (DEA00000509) Job Description Vice President for Principal Giving - ( DEA00000509 ) Description THE SEARCH Southern Methodist University (SMU) seeks a visionary, entrepreneurial, and highly strategic advancement leader to serve as its inaugural Vice President for Principal Giving (VP). This newly created position represents a significant investment in the future of philanthropy at SMU and reflects the university’s commitment to building a comprehensive principal gifts program capable of supporting its most ambitious academic and institutional priorities. Reporting to Senior Vice President for Development and External Affairs (SVP) Shontel Grumhaus, the VP will play a pivotal role in shaping the university’s philanthropic future while helping define the next era of fundraising excellence at SMU. Southern Methodist University is experiencing a period of extraordinary momentum and transformation. Under the leadership of President Jay Hartzell and Senior Vice President Grumhaus, the university is reimagining its advancement enterprise and making strategic investments in talent, infrastructure, and organizational capacity. As SMU prepares for its next chapter of campaign planning and philanthropic growth, university leadership recognizes a unique opportunity to elevate principal giving from a collection of individual efforts into a sophisticated, institution-wide strategy capable of securing transformational support for the university’s highest priorities. While SMU has achieved remarkable fundraising success and consistently outperformed expectations, much of its recent philanthropic growth has been driven through capital projects or other targeted initiatives. The university now seeks to build a more intentional principal giving program focused on advancing major academic priorities, supporting bold institutional initiatives, and cultivating the university’s most significant philanthropic partnerships. The institution is uniquely positioned to elevate its philanthropic trajectory. With ambitious academic priorities, a deeply engaged community of supporters, and significant untapped potential among its most generous constituents, SMU is poised to build a principal gift program of national distinction. The VP will have the rare opportunity to further establish and significantly elevate a principal gifts enterprise. Working closely with university leadership, deans, advancement colleagues, and key volunteer partners, the successful candidate will develop a comprehensive approach to identifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding donors capable of making transformational investments. The VP will serve as a strategic partner to the president and senior leadership team, help shape campaign priorities and philanthropic messaging, and establish the frameworks that connect donor interests to the university’s most impactful opportunities. The VP will also lead and develop a growing team of principal gift professionals, creating a culture of ambition, collaboration, and accountability while elevating the team’s capacity to pursue increasingly transformative philanthropic opportunities. This role arrives at a particularly exciting moment. SMU’s forthcoming strategic plan will articulate an ambitious vision for the institution’s future, accompanied by significant philanthropic priorities across multiple areas of the university. The VP will be instrumental in translating those aspirations into philanthropic priorities, building a sustainable principal gift pipeline, and helping position SMU for unprecedented and sustainable fundraising success. As a member of a collaborative and evolving leadership team, this individual will also serve as a thought partner in shaping campaign strategy, strengthening advancement operations, and building a culture of excellence, accountability, and innovation across the growing division. The successful candidate will be an accomplished principal gifts professional and collaborative leader who combines strategic vision with operational discipline. This individual will bring a record of securing transformational gifts, experience leading within complex organizations, and the ability to navigate confidently among highly influential donors, senior academic leaders, and institutional volunteers. Most importantly, the incoming VP will be energized by the opportunity to build, helping to establish the structures, partnerships, and philanthropic ambitions that will define principal giving at SMU for years to come. Southern Methodist University has retained Jack Gorman of the national executive search firm Isaacson, Miller to conduct this important recruitment. Confidential inquiries, nominations, and applications may be directed to the search firm as indicated at the end of this posting. ABOUT Southern Methodist University A nationally ranked private university located near the heart of Dallas, Southern Methodist University is a distinguished center for global research and teaching. SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the university’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study, and innovative programs. The university serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, Cox School of Business, Lyle School of Engineering, Meadows School of the Arts, Simmons School of Education and Human Development, Dedman School of Law, Perkins School of Theology, and Mood School of Graduate and Advanced Studies. SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the university to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty, and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers learning, research, social, and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. Fundraising and Financial Overview The Office of Development and External Affairs (DEA) comprises 147 professionals and supports Southern Methodist University by securing funding for university priorities; promoting meaningful involvement in the life and work of the university; heightening international, national, state, and local awareness of SMU; and supporting the enrollment of a diverse and able student body. In Fiscal Year 2026, SMU raised over $300 million in philanthropic support. From 2017 to 2025, SMU Ignited: Boldly Shaping Tomorrow – the university’s campaign for impact – inspired an era of unprecedented advancement in academics, athletics, and national prominence. Thanks to more than 67,500 donors, volunteers, and supporters, the campaign exceeded its goal three years ahead of schedule, raising over $1.656 billion and becoming one of the largest campaigns in the history of private Texas universities. KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES The Vice President for Principal Giving provides executive leadership for SMU’s principal giving strategy and serves as the university’s senior fundraising leader for transformational philanthropy. Reporting to Senior Vice President for Development and External Affairs Shontel Grumhaus, the VP partners closely with President Jay Hartzell, academic deans, trustees, volunteer leaders, and senior university leadership to secure the institution’s most significant philanthropic investments in support of the university’s strategic priorities. The VP represents SMU with donors, trustees, volunteer leaders, foundations, and external partners while serving as a trusted ambassador for the university’s mission and strategic vision. · Strategic Partnership with University Leadership: Advise senior leadership on philanthropic strategy, align donor interests with institutional priorities, and translate goals into transformational opportunities. Support board engagement, volunteer leadership recruitment, and briefing preparation. · Principal Gift Fundraising: Develop, manage, and personally lead a portfolio of some of the university’s highest-capacity prospects and donors by cultivating, soliciting, negotiating, and stewarding transformational gifts in close coordination with university leadership. · Presidential and Executive Partnership: Serve as the strategic fundraising partner to the President, SVP, deans, and academic leadership by developing donor engagement strategies, preparing presidential briefings, coordinating donor meetings, and providing strategic counsel on principal gift opportunities. · Presidential and University Events: Provide strategic leadership for presidential and university events involving principal gift prospects and donors, partnering with university leadership and advancement colleagues to leverage these opportunities for cultivation, engagmenet, solicitation, and stewardship. · Team Leadership and Staff Development: Build and scale a best-in-class principal giving program, fostering a culture of collaboration, accountability, and continuous improvement. Lead a team of five to six professionals, including: Senior Director of Principal Giving, Director of Principal Giving, Principal Gift Proposal Writer, Executive Assistant/Program Coordinator (to be hired), and additional Principal Gift Officers as the program grows. · Portfolio Strategy and Pipeline Management: Develop and oversee principal giving portfolio management processes, prospect strategy meetings, donor pipeline reviews, and fundraising analytics to maximize principal gift opportunities. · Campaign Leadership: In partnership with the SVP and VP for Campaign, provide leadership for comprehensive campaign planning and execution by developing transformational gift strategies aligned with campaign priorities and institutional objectives. · Cross-Functional Collaboration: Partner with key advancement units, including schools and unit development, corporate and foundation relations, gift planning, prospect development, stewardship, alumni engagement, parent and family philanthropy, and development services, to coordinate donor strategies and maximize fundraising success. · Proposal Development and Donor Communications: Oversee development of customized investment opportunities, principal gift proposals, case statements, presidential briefings, impact reports, donor communications, and stewardship strategies supporting transformational philanthropy. · Budget and Resource Management: Develop and manage departmental budgets, monitor fundraising performance, assess resource needs, and recommend organizational improvements to enhance effectiveness. APPLICATIONS, INQUIRIES, AND NOMINATIONS Screening of complete applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search process. Inquiries, nominations, referrals, and CVs with cover letters should be sent via the Isaacson, Miller website: Vice President for Principal Giving, Southern Methodist University Jack Gorman, Partner Nicole Poe, Managing Associate Lisa Clayton, Managing Search Coordinator Isaacson, Miller Southern Methodist University will not discriminate in any employment practice, education program, education activity, or admissions on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. SMU’s commitment to equal opportunity includes nondiscrimination on the basis of sexual orientation and gender identity and expression. This document has been prepared based on the information provided by Southern Methodist University. The material presented in this leadership profile should be relied on for informational purposes only. While every effort has been made to ensure the accuracy of this information, the original source documents and information provided by SMU would supersede any conflicting information in this document. Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS AND CHARACTERISTICS · A minimum of ten years of professional fundraising experience and at least seven years of management experience, preferably within a private research university setting. · Demonstrated success cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding seven- and eight-figure gifts and the ability to inspire high-net-worth individuals to provide significant philanthropic support to an institution. · Demonstrated experience strategically contributing to and guiding comprehensive campaigns, ideally from inception to successful close and post-campaign analysis. · The ability to influence without authority, navigate complex organizations, exercise executive presence and political acumen, translate academic priorities into philanthropic opportunities, and lead strategic change. · Professional credibility, intellectual depth, maturity, and collaborative skills to garner the trust and confidence of the President, Provost, deans, board members, faculty, and volunteer leaders. · The ability to lead, mentor, and motivate staff, and to encourage growth and change in both people and programs. A strong commitment to personal and professional development, and the ability to foster a collaborative and high-performing team environment. · Exemplary interpersonal communication skills; active, engaged listener; superior written and oral communication skills; clarity, crispness, and elegance in writing and speaking. The ability to succinctly articulate a vision and processes to key constituents, utilizing data and metrics as appropriate. · The desire and ability to build bridges and strong collaborative relationships with all members of an operation. · A sharp eye for operational efficiency and the best use of resources. · The ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and with confidence and discretion. · Impeccable attention to detail; superb follow-through; strong personal integrity, charisma, and work ethic coupled with a sense of humor and perspective. · The personal flexibility to travel and work evenings or weekends as required. · Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree preferred. Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Development Organization : DEA - Development/Alumni Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Executive Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : Yes, 25 % of the Time Job Posting : Sep 18, 2026, 12:38:41 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=MEA00000165": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Admissions & Recruiting (HR Title: Student Recruiter II)",
      "description": "Job Description - Associate Director, Admissions & Recruiting (HR Title: Student Recruiter II) (MEA00000165) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Associate Director, Admissions & Recruiting (HR Title: Student Recruiter II) (MEA00000165) Job Description Associate Director, Admissions & Recruiting (HR Title: Student Recruiter II) - ( MEA00000165 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Department: SMU Meadows School of the Arts educates visionary artists, scholars, and arts and communications professionals to create sustainable and transformative impact on both local and global society. Today, the Meadows School has achieved prominence as one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States and offers training in an unique mix of the arts – visual (art and art history), performing (dance, music, and theatre) and communications (advertising, film and media arts, corporate communication and public affairs, and journalism) – as well as a preeminent programs in arts management and arts entrepreneurship and creative computation. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The Associate Director of Recruiting & Admissions, Visual Arts, provides leadership for undergraduate and graduate visual art recruitment and admission within the Meadows School of the Arts. The position manages the prospective student experience from recruitment through enrollment, including auditions, admission review, scholarship strategy and yield efforts. In partnership with art, art history, film and advertising faculty and Meadows leadership, the Associate Director develops recruitment strategies and enrollment goals, analyzes results, advises on admission and scholarship decisions, and cultivates relationships that strengthen the quality and scope of the applicant pool. Essential Functions: Recruitment & Prospective Student Engagement: Lead recruitment efforts for undergraduate and graduate visual art programs and serve as a primary resource for prospective students and families. Develop recruitment initiatives and cultivate relationships with visual art/film educators, private teachers and other partners to expand and strengthen the applicant pool and improve enrollment yield. Admission & Enrollment Management: Oversee visual art admission processes and decisions in collaboration with faculty and Meadows leadership. Develop enrollment goals for studios and academic programs; monitor application, admission and enrollment results; identify emerging challenges; and recommend strategies to achieve enrollment objectives. Portfolio Reviews, Visits & Applicant Experience: Plan and oversee visual arts applications & pre-portfolio review activities and coordinate the prospective student experience, including campus visits, sample classes and  observations. Partner with faculty and staff to ensure effective communication, scheduling and a high-quality experience throughout recruitment and admission. Scholarship Strategy & Yield Management: Partner with scholarship and academic leadership to develop visual art scholarship strategies that support enrollment goals. Recommend allocations by evaluating faculty input, artistic and academic qualifications, financial aid circumstances, student interest and enrollment priorities, and monitor scholarship effectiveness in supporting yield. Recruitment Strategy, Communications & Outreach: Develop and assess targeted recruitment communications, outreach and promotional strategies for the visual arts. Partner with Meadows Marketing on digital and print materials, advertising and web content. Represent the visual arts at admission programs and college fairs and coordinate strategic participation by faculty, alumni and students. Admission Policy, Systems & Faculty Partnership: Partner with the visual arts departments and Meadows leadership to develop and maintain admission and audition policies, procedures and documentation. Improve processes and technology supporting admission and auditions; communicate requirements to faculty; and monitor developments and best practices in music admission to inform ongoing improvements. Student Transition, Team Support & Program Administration: Coordinate with Student Affairs on orientation, placement auditions, testing and related communications with entering students and faculty. Hire, train and supervise student workers supporting recruitment activities and provide operational coordination for Music recruitment and admission initiatives throughout the enrollment cycle. Some evening and weekend work during peak admission season may be required. Some travel may be required to attend conferences. Qualifications Education and Experience: Bachelor’s is required. Master's is preferred A degree field in music, communications or business is preferred. A minimum of three (3) years of experience working in higher education, music education, arts administration, student recruitment, admissions, or a related setting is required. Experience in collegiate music recruitment and/or admissions, including familiarity with music auditions, applicant review, recruitment events, prospective student engagement, or enrollment activities is preferred. Experience working collaboratively with music faculty is desired. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of constituencies such as, prospective students and their families, faculty, staff, and other participants in the admission process. A strong customer service orientation is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning, time management skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and detailed processes. An appreciation and understanding of students seeking enrollment in performing and visual arts programs is required. Knowledge of collegiate music programs and the needs of prospective students is preferred. Candidate must have a strong working knowledge of Microsoft Office, particularly in Outlook, Excel, and Word. Must also be able to learn new computer applications as the need arises. A valid driver's license is required. An understanding of recruitment and admissions data collection, analysis, and leveraging is preferred. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Bend, stand, kneel Reach above shoulders Push/pull Carry/lift 25 lbs. Deadline to Apply: Priority consideration may be granted to applications received by October 2, 2026. Deadline to apply is October 16, 2026. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Other Organization : Meadows School of the Arts Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 18, 2026, 6:48:34 AM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=PRO00000399": {
      "title": "Research Administrative Coordinator (HR Title: Administrative Specialist II)",
      "description": "Job Description - Research Administrative Coordinator (HR Title: Administrative Specialist II) (PRO00000399) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Research Administrative Coordinator (HR Title: Administrative Specialist II) (PRO00000399) Job Description Research Administrative Coordinator (HR Title: Administrative Specialist II) - ( PRO00000399 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The Office of Research & Innovation (ORI) at SMU is seeking a highly organized, proactive, and experienced Research Administrative Coordinator to provide advanced administrative, operational, programmatic, and research-support functions for a dynamic central research office. This position serves as a key operational partner within ORI, coordinating complex activities across research administration, events, communications, records management, and cross-unit initiatives. The role independently manages multiple priorities, serves as a trusted point of contact for faculty, staff, university partners, and external stakeholders, and handles sensitive and confidential information with discretion. The position also supports ORI leadership and operations through meeting coordination, documentation, project tracking, and follow-through on strategic and administrative initiatives that advance the University's research mission. Essential Functions: Research Administration & ORI Ops: Provide administrative and operational support for ORI and its four functional areas, including RIC, TTC, SPA, and Operations. Coordinate calendars, meetings, resources, workflows, inquiries, and projects. Serve as a key contact for faculty, staff, university offices, and external partners. Resolve issues, maintain documentation, and support efficient operations. Event Planning & Research Engagement: Provide administrative leadership for ORI research events, workshops, seminars, meetings, and engagement activities on and off campus. Develop timelines and coordinate speakers, invitations, registration, facilities, technology, materials, vendors, and event logistics. Support setup, day of coordinator and post-event follow-up. Executive & Operations Meetings: Provide administrative support to ORI leadership and Operations by coordinating meeting schedules, agendas, materials, and action items. Attend and document meetings, prepare accurate minutes, capture decisions and assigned responsibilities, and distribute meeting documentation. Track outstanding action items and coordinate follow-up with responsible parties. Communications, Projects & Cross-Functional Initiatives: Support ORI communications, special projects, and cross-functional initiatives. Draft and distribute professional communications and support newsletters, website content, flyers, presentations, and outreach materials. Track project milestones, deliverables, and metrics; coordinate with university partners; assist with the implementation of new processes and systems; and independently manage assigned projects from planning through completion. Office Operations & Efficiency: Manage office supply inventory, coordinate with the Executive Assistant to enhance office efficiency, conduct building inspections with students, greet and assist visitors, coordinate room setups, and maintain effective office workflows to support daily operations, organization, safety, and a welcoming, efficient environment for staff, students, and visitors. Occasional Outside-of-Business-Hours Events & Logistics Support: Provide before- and after-hours event support, including travel to pick up supplies and transport materials to research or satellite locations. Occasional travel to off-campus locations may also be required, including transporting materials or assisting with event setup. Other Duties as Assigned: Support additional research administration, operational, and departmental activities as needed to advance ORI priorities and ensure effective office operations. Qualifications A Bachelor’s degree is required, preferably in Business Administration or a related field. A minimum of three (3) years of administrative or project coordination experience is required, preferably in higher education or research administration. Experience in event planning and logistical coordination preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and develop and maintain collaborative relationships with a wide range of constituencies. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. A strong customer service orientation and a collaborative approach is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have strong organizational, planning and time-management skills. Strong project coordination skills, effective meeting management, and documentation skills are essential. Candidate must demonstrate strong proficiency in the Microsoft Office Suite. A strong working knowledge of and/or experience with database tools and virtual meeting platforms (e.g., Microsoft Teams and Zoom) is also required. S trong records and data management skills preferred. Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgment, maintain confidentiality, and handle sensitive information with discretion and professionalism is required. Physical and Environmental Demands: Bend Reach above shoulder level Carry/Lift up to 25 lbs. Sit for extended periods Squat Handle objects (dexterity) Carry/Lift over 25 -50 lbs. Kneel Stand Walk for extended periods or long distances Push/Pull Crawl Climb Carry/Lift over 50 lbs. Deadline to Apply: October 8, 2026 Priority consideration may be given to submissions received by September 27, 2026. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Office and Administrative Support Organization : Provost - Other Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 17, 2026, 2:43:52 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    },
    "https://smu.taleo.net/careersection/ex/jobdetail.ftl?job=CON00000039": {
      "title": "Unit Financial Planning Officer",
      "description": "Job Description - Unit Financial Planning Officer (CON00000039) Go to the main content section. Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Job Cart | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Jobpage Basic Search | Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Unit Financial Planning Officer (CON00000039) Job Description Unit Financial Planning Officer - ( CON00000039 ) Description Salary Range: Salary commensurate with experience and qualifications About SMU SMU’s more than 12,000 diverse, high-achieving students come from all 50 states and over 80 countries to take advantage of the University’s small classes, meaningful research opportunities, leadership development, community service, international study and innovative programs. SMU serves approximately 7,000 undergraduates and 5,000 graduate students through eight degree-granting schools: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences , Cox School of Business , Lyle School of Engineering , Meadows School of the Arts , Simmons School of Education and Human Development , Dedman School of Law , Perkins School of Theology and Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies . SMU is data driven, and its powerful supercomputing ecosystem – paired with entrepreneurial drive – creates an unrivaled environment for the University to deliver research excellence. Now in its second century of achievement, SMU is recognized for the ways it supports students, faculty and alumni as they become ethical, enterprising leaders in their professions and communities. SMU’s relationship with Dallas – the dynamic center of one of the nation’s fastest-growing regions – offers unique learning, research, social and career opportunities that provide a launch pad for global impact. SMU is nonsectarian in its teaching and committed to academic freedom and open inquiry. About the Department: The Office of Finance and Planning (OFP) supports the University's mission by safeguarding assets, maintaining fiscal integrity, and encouraging the rational and effective allocation of the University’s financial resources About the Position: This role is an on-campus, in-person position. The Unit Financial Planning Officer (FPO) provides high-level financial, administrative, and strategic planning support to the unit executive leader to position the Unit meet key performance metrics and strategic goals. The role analyzes complex data sets and helps the executive leader to understand the data and make data-informed decisions that align with the unit’s strategic plan and optimally contributes to University’s overall strategic plan. Provides oversight of financial planning, resource allocation, business operations, and budget forecasting for the unit and its underlying departments. The position also works with the University Planning team to develop advanced business and financial models, data analyses, and process improvements in support of unit’s goals and growth. The FPO provides leadership to the unit and department staff related to the college business functions. Job level and compensation will be determined based on the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications. Essential Functions: Work with complex data sets, analytics tools, and visualization software to analyze data and help the executive leader to understand the data and make data-informed decisions that align with the unit’s strategic plan and key performance metrics. This includes leading the unit to understand the revenue and cost margins of each department, program, course, and activity, and identifying opportunities for strategic optimization of resources. Work closely with the Unit executive leader and provides oversight to the departments on financial planning, resource allocation, grants and contracts, business operations, enrollment management, endowments and gifts, scholarships, waivers, GTA / GRA appointments, salary, and benefit allocations, compliance reporting, ranking surveys, budget forecasting, and data needs. Develop advanced business and financial models for enrollment and revenue projections, evaluate academic programs and new revenue-generating opportunities, models research productivity, workforce planning, faculty hiring, infrastructure assessments, and space needs, and strategically plans for rapidly evolving opportunities and challenges for the unit and its departments. Work with databases and create new reports as needed. Understand the financial condition of the unit and its departments, as well as the university, and formulate financially sustainable strategies and contingencies for the unit. Continuously assesses and monitors financial performance and forecasts budget to actuals. Represent the unit in business and financial meetings and on University committees. Serve as a liaison between the unit and the Strategic Financial Planning department of the Office of Finance & Planning and that keeps that office informed of challenges and opportunities. Collaborate with the directors of the unit team to strengthen and improve business processes within the units, incorporating and sharing university best practices. Communicates and provides consultative training and guidance to the department chairs and business and administrative staff to ensure they understand the finances of the department and use consistent and effective business practices within the college. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree required. Master’s degree preferred. Degree in in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, IT, or a business-related field with emphasis placed on accounting or finance education backgrounds. A minimum of five years of progressive experience in Financial Planning & Analysis roles, preferably with some experience in a higher education setting. Financial consultative experience to either client principals or executive leadership within an organization preferred. Experience analyzing complex data sets and creating graphs and visual formats that allow the unit executive leader and administrators to quickly understand the analysis desired. Experience creating financial models, using databases and AI, and in creating informative reports and dashboards to inform decision-making is a plus. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Candidate must demonstrate strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate broadly across the University and serve as a trusted advisor to senior leadership on financial, operational, and strategic matters. Must also demonstrate strong written communication skills. Skill in presenting complex financial and operational information into actionable recommendations for executive leadership and influence decision-making to positive outcomes is essential. Candidate must possess strong problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and analyze problems, as well as devise solutions. Must also have the ability to align resource planning and financial strategies with institutional and unit priorities. The ability to identify opportunities for growth, efficiency, and optimization of resources is essential. Candidate must have strong organizational, planning and time-management skills. A keen attention to detail and accuracy is essential. Candidate must have skill in developing dashboards, reports, and executive-level presentations that simplify complex information required. Must also have an intermediate to advanced level with Microsoft Excel. Data analysis and integrity skills and ability to conjoin data from multiple systems is essential. Experience with ERP systems, FP&A systems and reporting tools strongly preferred. Power BI, Tableau, or similar analytics and visualization platforms desired. Physical and Environmental Demands: Sit for long periods of time Deadline to Apply: The position is open until filled. EEO Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer.  All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Benefits: SMU offers staff a broad, competitive array of health and related benefit s. In addition to traditional benefits such as health, dental, and vision plans, SMU offers a wide range of wellness programs to help attract, support, and retain our employees whose work continues to make SMU an outstanding education and research institution. SMU is committed to providing an array of retirement programs that benefit and protect you and your family throughout your working years at SMU and, if you meet SMU's retirement eligibility criteria, during your retirement years after you leave SMU. The value of learning at SMU isn't just about preparing our students for the future. Employees have access to a wide variety of professional and personal development opportunities , including tuition benefits . Primary Location : USA-TX-Dallas Job : Finance and Accounting Organization : Controller's Office Schedule : Regular Shift : Staff Employee Status : Individual Contributor Job Type : Full-time Job Level : Day Job Travel : No Job Posting : Sep 17, 2026, 12:04:23 PM © Southern Methodist University, PO Box 750100, Dallas, TX 75275"
    }
  },
